Pneumatic atomization
The pneumatic atomization, also referred as twin-fluid atomization, consists of the
use of an atomizing air flow, which is aimed to increase the relative velocities over
the external surface of the liquid phase, and therefore its disintegration. The
technique is referred as air-assisted atomization if it involves relative small quantities
of air on high velocities, as air-blasted atomization otherwise [12].
The main established and studied technologies, as reviewed in [13], consist either of
the use of several air jets, which increase the liquid atomization through its shaping
into a liquid sheet (air-blasted, external mixing atomization), or of an internal mixing
resulting in the injection of the two fluids from the same nozzle (air-assisted, internal
mixing atomization).
As observed for the atomization of an isolated jet in regimen of wind-induced
breakup, the effectiveness of the technique is represented through the Weber
number, which presents a proportionality with the square of the relative speed
between the two fluids: We ∝ u2R
In practice, the influence of the relative speed on the droplet size also depends on
the atomizer configuration, in the form of SMD ∝ uRA , with the A value on the
exponent spanning - in extent to the previous contributions reviewed in [14] 
between 0.4 and 1.33. The Air-to-Fuel Ratio (AFR) - the dimensionless ratio between
the air and fuel flow rates - is also an important variable for the atomization, as the
effectiveness of the atomizing air flow results defined between the threshold values
AFRMIN and AFRMAX. For values below AFRMIN, the effect of the air flow is negligible as
the flow is insufficient to overcome the viscous and surface tensions opposing the
disintegration of the liquid phase. Instead, the extension over AFRMAX provides a
limited improvement on the atomization, as the additional air does not participate
to the breakup process. The threshold values also depend on the atomizer
configuration.
Both the air-assisted and the air-blasted methods require a large amount of air to
achieve a good atomization. Previous experiments operated with air blasted water
achieved a SMD of 75 μm with an AFR of the order of ~1 [15]. Experimental studies
on swirl air blasted atomizers showed that an SMD of the order 50 μm can be
achieved at a AFR of ~8 [16].


