Droplet size distribution measurements and imaging
The main aim of the atomization process is to increase the specific surface of the
liquid phase, in order to achieve high evaporation rates. In practice, the process
results in a range of droplet sizes, spanning from the μm to the mm, depending on
the atomizer and on the properties of the liquid phase.
The performances of the designed injector and the selected fuels have been
investigated through the measurement of the distribution of the droplet size. The
characterization has been operated with the Malvern Particle Sizer 2600, a
commercial instrument operating an optical technique based on the Fraunhofer
diffraction of monochromatic light. The experiments have been conducted with
different conditions, in terms of injector head configuration and position, and in
terms of involved fuel and air flows. The results of the different measurements are
compared on their distribution mean value, variance and quantiles.
The atomization mechanism in the proximities of the impingement point has also
been evaluated through the use of a Phantom V4.2 HSC (High-Speed Camera), as
the development of this region affects the development of the whole atomizing jet,
included the sample region for the size distribution characterization.
The exposure time has been set on the minimum value (10-6 s), in order to capture
the development of the separating ligaments and droplet in high definition, no
matter their speed.
