Influence of the fuel
As mentioned in 2.2, not all the properties of the AJF are known, and the discussion
over the influence of the fuels (and their properties) over the atomization results
therefore limited.
The use of JF results, for both the aligned and the misaligned conditions, in an
improved atomization, as the conventional fuel properties result more suitable for
the process.
The different behaviour of the two fuels is due, in greater extent, to the difference
between their viscosities. For this reason, the better performances of the JF should
not surprise, as its viscosity is estimated, for the experimental ambient temperature
around 24°, of ~1.30 mm2/s, against the ~2.30 mm2/s estimated for AJF.
The approximation over the fuels viscosities appears appropriate through the
calculation of the fuels Oh number (in which a further estimated value is introduced,
as the AJF surface tension is unknown):
Oh JF = ~1.1∙10-2
Oh AJF = ~1.7∙10-2
The values are in agreement with the shapes assumed for NJ = 2, in airless
conditions, Figure 3.14. The JF appears shaped as a ruffled flapping sheet, while the
AJF appears in development from open rim sheet (even though the opened portion
is not visible in the picture) and the smooth flapping sheet, as it is possible to detect
the waves propagating on the flat surface.
If compared with the atomization in misaligned conditions on the same fuel (JF) and
for all the three atomizer configuration (NJ), the atomization results improved for
the minimum values of ṁF, and presents conditions of fuel concentration and
conceal for the maximum values. The formation of the conceal appears to manifests
in a greater extent with JF than with AJF, which suggests a relationship of the
phenomenon with the fuel density, which is higher for JF.
If further compared with the atomization of AJF, the atomization of JF increases the
positive effect of the alignment, and leads, in conditions of excess air, to a SMD up
to 5 μm (NJ = 2 and 4) or up to 10 μm (NJ = 3) smaller.


