4 Closure
Conclusions
The simultaneous characterization of both an alternative fuel for aeronautical
applications and an innovative atomization technique, consisting of the combined
atomizing action of an assisting air stream and of the multiple fuel jets on each other,
has been conducted.
The experimental activities have been operated with an alternative blend, consisting
of conventional jet fuel and a commercial green diesel, presenting promising traits
for the use in aeronautical engines. The experiments have also been operated on
plain jet fuel, in order to allow a comparison between the conventional and the
alternative solutions. The atomizer has been designed and manufactured for the
purpose, as no commercial model with the required configuration has been found
available on market.
The performances of both the alternative fuel and the innovative technique have
been evaluated through the measurement of the droplet size distribution resulting
from the atomization process. The measurements have been acquired through a
non-invasive optical technique. Further investigations on the breakup mechanisms
have been conducted through the use of an high-speed camera, aimed to capture
the motion of the single droplets and ligaments in the proximities of the
impingement point.
The measurements showed good results for the innovative atomization technique,
as it allows to reach, in extent to the atomization of JF in the non-reactive conditions
of interest, a SMD of the order of ~20 μm with an AFR value of ~0.20.
The conditions of excess of air do not present a strong dependence on the fuel
nozzle alignment, even though the greater precision results in an improvement,
which appears as more intense for the JF than for the AJF. In conditions of excess of
fuel, the jet impingement can have an either positive or negative effect on the
atomization, as it develops in structures presenting an either greater or smaller
resistance to the action of the atomizing air stream. The use of an optimal fuel mass
flow, which value appears to depend, on both the atomizer configuration and the
atomized fuel, results in the maximisation of the combined effect of the atomizing
techniques, as these conditions allow the most efficient employment of the liquid
phase momentum for the atomization.


Closure
74
The experimental results show that the increase of the involved fuel worsens the
atomization, in greater extent if the increase is operated through the addition of
further fuel nozzles. It is worth to notice that the use of a greater number of fuel
nozzles results in a higher SMD, but allows the atomization of a greater amount of
fuel without the need of an intensification of the fuel pressure (and therefore of a
heavier fuel pump).
The atomization of AJF results in higher SMD values than the atomization of JF, as
the different fuel properties result in a stronger resistance to the atomization for
both the pneumatic technique, the multiple impingement technique and their
combined use. The difference in the resulting SMD, however, is limited to small
values, and is therefore promising for the future development of this alternative fuel
in the avionic sector, even though further investigations are required to assess if it
is compatible with the whole aeronautical engine, and not just with its injection and
atomization stage.
Future work
An extension of the experimental activities towards the reactive conditions would
allow a wider understanding of the overall effect of both the use of the synthetic
hydrocarbon compounds and the innovative atomizing technique. It would allow to
understand, for example, if the positive effect of the higher LHV of the alternative
blending component can overcome its negative effect on the atomization.
Further experiments operated with different fuels, with different impingement
angles and with different jet velocities (different nozzle diameters) could allow a
wider understanding over the breakup phenomenon, and reach the identification of
the optimum conditions for the atomizing technique.


